  "item": "57",
  "target": "breakdown (epics E1-E2, stories S1-S5) for DLQ operator endpoints",
  "verdict": "WEAKENED",
  "attacks": [
    {
      "attack": "Coverage: does every requirement in the item body map to a story?",
      "method": "Walked each clause of the body (browse with paging, inspect payload and failure reason, redrive selected, redrive all, idempotent, no disturbance of in-flight delivery) against S1-S5.",
      "result": "All clauses are covered: browse is S1, inspect is S2, selected redrive is S3, redrive-all is S4, idempotency and in-flight safety are S5. No requirement is dropped."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Testability: is every acceptance criterion verifiable and specific?",
      "method": "Read each AC's 'then' clause and asked whether a test could pass or fail on it without further interpretation.",
      "result": "S3 AC1 fails. Its 'then' is 'the feature works correctly', which is tautological and defines no observable outcome. The happy path of selective redrive (selected messages are re-enqueued on the subscription, and are no longer listed as dead-lettered) is never stated. The other ACs are concrete."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Edge cases in paging",
      "method": "Checked S1 ACs for ordering, page size bounds, invalid or expired cursors, and behavior when the DLQ changes between page requests.",
      "result": "AC2 says 'no duplicates' but nothing about omissions or stable ordering. A concurrent redrive or new dead-letter between page requests could skip items. An invalid or tampered paging token has no AC. The page-size limit ('bounded') has no stated maximum or default."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Idempotency definition (S5 AC1)",
      "method": "Asked what 'identical redrive request' means and what the response is on repeat.",
      "result": "Idempotency is defined only for repeats of the same request. No mechanism is implied (idempotency key vs. state-based). The more natural case, where a message was already redriven and is no longer in the DLQ, collides with S3 AC2, which says an id not in the DLQ is rejected. So the repeat of a successful redrive is either rejected or a no-op, and the breakdown does not say which. These two ACs are in tension."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Redrive semantics and failure modes",
      "method": "Looked for ACs on partial failure, ordering, retry-count reset, and the outcome when a redriven message dead-letters again.",
      "result": "Missing: atomicity of a multi-id redrive (S3 AC2 covers rejection of the whole request on an unknown id but not enqueue failures), whether the delivery-attempt count resets, and that a message which fails again returns to the DLQ without being lost. Redrive-all on a very large DLQ has no bound or batching story."
    },
    {
      "attack": "Cross-cutting concerns",
      "method": "Checked for authorization, auditing, and unknown-subscription handling.",
      "result": "These are operator endpoints exposing payloads and mutating delivery state, yet there is no AC on operator authorization, no audit record of redrives, and no AC for a nonexistent subscription (S1 AC3 covers only an empty DLQ). S2 should also probably scope the id to the subscription."
    }
  ],
  "findings": [
    {
      "finding": "S3 AC1 is vacuous ('the feature works correctly') and must be rewritten to state the observable outcome.",
      "evidence": "S3 AC1 then-clause: 'the feature works correctly'. This is the core happy path of the redrive feature and is currently untestable."
    },
    {
      "finding": "S3 AC2 and S5 AC1 conflict on repeated redrives of already-redriven ids.",
      "evidence": "S3 AC2 rejects ids that are not dead-lettered on the subscription. S5 AC1 requires a repeated identical request not to re-enqueue. After the first success the ids are no longer dead-lettered, so the repeat is rejected under S3 AC2, which contradicts the idempotent no-op that 'idempotent' implies. The expected response needs to be defined."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Paging AC allows silent omissions and has no invalid-cursor or page-size behavior.",
      "evidence": "S1 AC2 only asserts 'no duplicates'. No AC covers a stable order, a malformed cursor, or a maximum page size."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Failure and security behavior of redrive is unspecified.",
      "evidence": "No AC covers partial enqueue failure, re-dead-lettering after redrive, unknown subscription, operator authorization, or auditing, though the item describes operator endpoints that expose payloads and mutate state."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Coverage and decomposition are otherwise sound.",
      "evidence": "Every body clause maps to a story, and the stories are small and backend-only, consistent with the triage (feature, standard path, no architecture or breaking change). The defects are in AC quality, not scope."
    }
  ]
}
```

